sec.p:
The proposed neural circuit comprises four distinct cell types: the principal neurons (y), the modulatory excitatory neurons (u), the modulatory inhibitory neurons (a and q), with the following dynamics:
(1a)
τy1dy1dt=−y1+β12⊙z1+(11+a1+)(W11y1++γ12⊙W12y2+) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \tau_{y_{1}} \frac{\mathrm{~d} \mathbf{y}_{1}}{\mathrm{~d} t} & =-\mathbf{y}_{1}+\frac{\beta_{1}}{2} \odot \mathbf{z}_{1} \\ & +\left(\frac{1}{1+\mathbf{a}_{1}^{+}}\right)\left(\mathbf{W}_{11} \sqrt{\mathbf{y}_{1}^{+}}+\frac{\gamma_{1}}{2} \odot \mathbf{W}_{12} \sqrt{\mathbf{y}_{2}^{+}}\right) \tag{(1a} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
(1b)
τu1du1dt=−u1+(σ12)2+N1(y1+⊙u1+2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{u_{1}} \frac{\mathrm{~d} \mathbf{u}_{1}}{\mathrm{~d} t}=-\mathbf{u}_{1}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{1}}{2}\right)^{2}+\mathbf{N}_{1}\left(\mathbf{y}_{1}^{+} \odot \mathbf{u}_{1}^{+2}\right) \tag{$(1 \mathrm{~b}$} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(1c)
τa1da1dt=−a1+W12y2+2⋅q1++u1++a1⊙u1++α1du1dt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{a_{1}} \frac{\mathrm{~d} \mathbf{a}_{1}}{\mathrm{~d} t}=-\mathbf{a}_{1}+\frac{\mathbf{W}_{12} \sqrt{\mathbf{y}_{2}^{+}}}{2 \cdot \mathbf{q}_{1}^{+}}+\mathbf{u}_{1}^{+}+\mathbf{a}_{1} \odot \mathbf{u}_{1}^{+}+\alpha_{1} \frac{\mathrm{~d} \mathbf{u}_{1}}{\mathrm{~d} t} \tag{1c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(1d)
τq1dq1dt=−q1+y1+. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{q_{1}} \frac{\mathrm{~d} \mathbf{q}_{1}}{\mathrm{~d} t}=-\mathbf{q}_{1}+\sqrt{\mathbf{y}_{1}^{+}} . \tag{(1d} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The above equations are for V1; V2 follows a similar set of equations (see SI Appendix). The membrane potentials of neurons in cortical areas V1 and V2 are denoted by y1 and y2, respectively, while their corresponding firing rates are represented by y1+\begin{document}$\mathbf{y}_{1}^{+}$\end{document} and y2+\begin{document}$\mathbf{y}_{2}^{+}$\end{document}. The ⊙ symbol represents the Hadamard product, element-wise multiplication of vectors. To estimate the firing rates y1+\begin{document}$\mathbf{y}_{1}^{+}$\end{document} and y2+\begin{document}$\mathbf{y}_{2}^{+}$\end{document} from their respective membrane potentials, we employ a modified Gaussian Rectification (GR) model [94, 95]. The variable z1 denotes the input drive to V1 from the preceding brain area (the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, LGN), computed as a weighted (Wzx) sum of the LGN firing rates (Fig. 7a illustrates the resulting orientation tuning curves). τk is the time constant for each subtype of neuron, where k=y,u,a,q⋅W11y1+\begin{document}$\mathbf{k}=\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{u}, \mathbf{a}, \mathbf{q} \cdot \mathbf{W}_{11} \sqrt{\mathbf{y}_{1}^{+}}$\end{document} and W12y2+\begin{document}$\mathbf{W}_{12} \sqrt{\mathbf{y}_{2}^{+}}$\end{document} are the recurrent and feedback drive, respectively, where W11 is the recurrent weight matrix for V1. W11 has a center-surround structure where the closer connections are excitatory and the more distant connections are inhibitory (Fig. 7b), same as in [7]. The recurrent weight matrix for V2, W22, has a similar structure. The maximum eigenvalue for these recurrent weight matrices is set to unity to ensure stability. However, we have shown that this need not be the case because normalization ensures stability [8, 9]. W12 represents the inter-areal feedback connectivity matrix from V2 to V1; it is diagonally dominant, with net excitatory entries near the diagonal and weak net inhibitory entries farther from it (Fig. 7c). The feedforward matrix from V1 to V2, W21, is taken to be the transpose of W12. Given that W12 is symmetric, it follows that W12 = W21. The normalization matrix in V1 (N1) and V2 (N2) is taken to be all ones, meaning each neuron contributes equally to the normalization. All of these matrices describe effective connectivity; negative weights may, for example, be implemented with disynaptic inhibition through interneurons that are not modeled explicitly (see Discussion). The input gain and feedback gain for V1 are modulated by β1 and γ1, respectively. The recurrent and feedback amplifications in V1 are modulated dynamically by the inhibitory neuron population a1.

The time evolution of neural population activity in V1 and V2 can be described by a general stochastic differential equation (SDE) with an additive noise term LdW:
(4)
dx=f(x,u)dt+LdW, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{d} \mathbf{x}=\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \mathrm{d} t+\mathbf{L} \mathrm{d} \mathbf{W}, \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where x ∈ ℝn is a vector representing the activity of neurons; f(x, u) ∈ ℝn represents the deterministic part of the dynamics explained above (Eq. 1); dW is a vector of independent Gaussian increments with correlation matrix D (E[dW · dWT]), and L ∈ ℝn×n is the dispersion matrix defining how noise enters the system. Given the fixed point of the dynamical system, we can linearize the system around the fixed point (x∗):
(5)
dx=Jx∗(x−x∗)dt+LdW, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{d} \mathbf{x}=\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{x}^{*}}\left(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}^{*}\right) \mathrm{d} t+\mathbf{L} \mathrm{d} \mathbf{W}, \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Jx∗ is the Jacobian computed at the fixed point x∗. This linearization leads to a wide-sense stationary Gaussian process, x(t), whose power spectral density matrix, S(ω) ∈ ℝn×n, is given by [96]:
(6)
S(ω)=E[|x^(ω)|2]=(ıωI+J)−1LDL⊤(−ıωI+J)−⊤ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{S}(\omega)=\mathbb{E}\left[|\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\omega)|^{2}\right]=(\imath \omega \mathbf{I}+\mathbf{J})^{-1} \mathbf{L D L}^{\top}(-\imath \omega \mathbf{I}+\mathbf{J})^{-\top} \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
From S(ω), we can directly compute the coherence, κij, between any two i and j neurons in the two areas:
(7)
κij=|Sij|2SiiSjj. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \kappa_{i j}=\frac{\left|\mathcal{S}_{i j}\right|^{2}}{\mathcal{S}_{i i} \mathcal{S}_{j j}} . \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The linearization was validated by direct numerical integration of the full nonlinear stochastic system, including all noise sources, with the Euler–Maruyama scheme (time step 10 µs; 100 independent trials of 16 s after a 1 s burn-in, at each contrast); power and cross-spectra were estimated from the simulated trajectories by Welch’s method (4 s segments, 50% overlap) and compared with Eqs. 6 and 7 (Suppl. Fig. S6; SI Appendix, “Numerical validation of the analytical spectra”). A quantitative comparison with experimental spectra, in which the model parameters are fitted, is described in the SI Appendix (“Quantitative fit to experimental power and coherence spectra”) and shown in Suppl. Fig. S7.

The fluctuations in the responses and their co-variability are fully determined by the correlation matrix at steady-state when the strength of the noise is small. Therefore the correlation matrix, denoted as C(0), for the responses x(t) can be computed by solving the Lyapunov equation [96]:
(8)
JC(0)+C(0)J⊤=−LDL⊤. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{J C}(0)+\mathbf{C}(0) \mathbf{J}^{\top}=-\mathbf{L} \mathbf{D} \mathbf{L}^{\top} . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Once C(0) is obtained, we follow the method proposed by Semedo et al. [15] to construct an analytical model of the communication subspace. To achieve this, we divide the mean-subtracted responses of the entire neuronal population, denoted as x, into two subsets: s (responses of the source neurons, e.g., in V1) and t (responses of the target neurons, e.g., in V2 or V1). We consider a linear model for predicting target responses using the source responses, t^=B⊤s\begin{document}$\hat{\mathbf{t}}=\mathbf{B}^{\top} \mathbf{s}$\end{document}, where the mean squared error (MSE) is given by:
(9)
ε=E[|t−B⊤s|2]=Tr(C2+B⊤C1B−2B⊤C3), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \varepsilon=\mathbb{E}\left[\left|\mathbf{t}-\mathbf{B}^{\top} \mathbf{s}\right|^{2}\right]=\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}+\mathbf{B}^{\top} \mathbf{C}_{1} \mathbf{B}-2 \mathbf{B}^{\top} \mathbf{C}_{3}\right), \tag{(9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where C1 = E[ssT], C2 = E[ttT], and C3 = E[stT]. These matrices can be directly obtained from C(0). To find the optimal readout matrix Bopt, we minimize ε with respect to B, leading to the Ordinary Least Squares solution:
(10)
Bopt=C1−1C3, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{B}_{\text {opt }}=\mathbf{C}_{1}^{-1} \mathbf{C}_{3}, \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
First, we define the covariance of the target activity predicted by the optimal model (note the “hats”), which we denote as Ĉ2:
(11)
C^2=E[t^t^⊤]=Bopt⊤C1Bopt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\mathbf{C}}_{2}=\mathbb{E}\left[\hat{\mathbf{t}} \hat{\mathbf{t}}^{\top}\right]=\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{opt}}^{\top} \mathbf{C}_{1} \mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{opt}} \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
By substituting the expression for Bopt, we arrive at a key relationship:
(12)
C^2=(C1−1C3)⊤C1(C1−1C3)=C3⊤C1−1C3 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\mathbf{C}}_{2}=\left(\mathbf{C}_{1}^{-1} \mathbf{C}_{3}\right)^{\top} \mathbf{C}_{1}\left(\mathbf{C}_{1}^{-1} \mathbf{C}_{3}\right)=\mathbf{C}_{3}^{\top} \mathbf{C}_{1}^{-1} \mathbf{C}_{3} \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The principal dimensions of this predicted covariance matrix, Ĉ2, define the communication subspace. These are found by computing the eigen-decomposition:
(13)
C^2=VΛV⊤ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\mathbf{C}}_{2}=\mathbf{V} \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \mathbf{V}^{\top} \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, the columns of the orthogonal matrix V are the eigenvectors of Ĉ2 and Λ is a diagonal matrix of the corresponding eigenvalues, sorted in descending order. The prediction performance for a reduced-rank approximation of dimension i is defined as: A rank-i approximation, BRRR, is constructed by projecting the optimal solution Bopt onto the subspace spanned by the top i eigenvectors of Ĉ2.
(14)
BRRR(i)=BoptV(i)V⊤(i)=BoptPi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{RRR}}(i)=\mathbf{B}_{\text {opt }} \mathbf{V}(i) \mathbf{V}^{\top}(i)=\mathbf{B}_{\text {opt }} \mathbf{P}_{i} \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where V(i) is a matrix containing first i columns of V. Here Pi = V(i)VT(i) is the projection matrix. Note that Pi is symmetric (Pi⊤=Pi)\begin{document}$\left(\mathbf{P}_{i}^{\top}=\mathbf{P}_{i}\right)$\end{document} and idempotent (Pi2=Pi)\begin{document}$\left(\mathbf{P}_{i}^{2}=\mathbf{P}_{i}\right)$\end{document}. We define the prediction performance as:
(15)
Performance(i)=1−εiε0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \text { Performance }(i)=1-\frac{\varepsilon_{i}}{\varepsilon_{0}}, \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where εi is the MSE for the rank-i approximation
(16)
εi=Tr(C2+BRRR⊤(i)C1BRRR(i)−2BRRR⊤(i)C3). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \varepsilon_{i}=\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}+\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{RRR}}^{\top}(i) \mathbf{C}_{1} \mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{RRR}}(i)-2 \mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{RRR}}^{\top}(i) \mathbf{C}_{3}\right) . \tag{(16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
ε0 is the MSE for the case where the readout matrix is a null matrix, corresponding to the total variance of the target population (ε0 = Tr(C2)).

To simplify the expressions for εi. We substitute BRRR(i) = BoptPi into the expression:
(17)
εi=Tr(C2)−Tr(2PiBopt⊤C3−PiBopt⊤C1BoptPi) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \varepsilon_{i}=\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\right)-\operatorname{Tr}\left(2 \mathbf{P}_{i} \mathbf{B}_{\text {opt }}^{\top} \mathbf{C}_{3}-\mathbf{P}_{i} \mathbf{B}_{\text {opt }}^{\top} \mathbf{C}_{1} \mathbf{B}_{\text {opt }} \mathbf{P}_{i}\right) \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Now, we substitute Bopt⊤=C3⊤C1−1\begin{document}$\mathbf{B}_{\text {opt }}^{\top}=\mathbf{C}_{3}^{\top} \mathbf{C}_{1}^{-1}$\end{document} and recognize that the term C^2\begin{document}$\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{2}$\end{document} is Ĉ2. Using the cyclic property of the trace (Tr(AB) = Tr(BA)) and the idempotent property of the projection matrix (Pi2=Pi)\begin{document}$\left(\mathbf{P}_{i}^{2}=\mathbf{P}_{i}\right)$\end{document}, we can simplify further:
(18)
εi=Tr(C2)−Tr(C^2Pi) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \varepsilon_{i}=\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\right)-\operatorname{Tr}\left(\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{2} \mathbf{P}_{i}\right) \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
By applying the cyclic property and expanding Pi, we can see that the second term is the sum of the top i eigenvalues (λj) of Ĉ2.
(19)
Tr(C^2V(i)V(i)⊤)=Tr(V(i)⊤C^2V(i))=∑j=1iλj \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{Tr}\left(\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{2} \mathbf{V}(i) \mathbf{V}(i)^{\top}\right)=\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{V}(i)^{\top} \hat{\mathbf{C}}_{2} \mathbf{V}(i)\right)=\sum_{j=1}^{i} \lambda_{j} \tag{19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Now the expression for prediction performance simplifies to:
(20)
Performance(i)=∑j=1iλjTr(C2), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{Performance}(i)=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{i} \lambda_{j}}{\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\right)}, \tag{20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Which can also be interpreted as:
(21)
Performance(i)=Explained variance(idimensions) Total target variance . \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \text { Performance }(i)=\frac{\text { Explained variance }(i \text { dimensions })}{\text { Total target variance }} . \tag{21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The maximum prediction performance (full-rank approximation) is given by:
(22)
Performance=Tr(C^2)Tr(C2)=Tr(C3TC1−1C3)Tr(C2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \text { Performance }=\frac{\operatorname{Tr}\left(\hat{\mathbf{C}}_{2}\right)}{\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\right)}=\frac{\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{C}_{3}^{\mathrm{T}} \mathbf{C}_{1}^{-1} \mathbf{C}_{3}\right)}{\operatorname{Tr}\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\right)} \tag{22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
For the communication-subspace analyses (Figs. 2g and 3k,l), source and target populations of 30 principal neurons were drawn at random from the 72 neurons of each area, and prediction performance and dimensionality were averaged over random draws. The dependence of the dimensionality on the number of modeled and sampled neurons is characterized in Suppl. Fig. S9.

To analyze how communication between different brain areas varies across frequencies, we consider the frequency decomposition of the neural responses. The Wiener-Khinchin theorem states that the autocorrelation function C(τ) is the inverse Fourier transform of the power spectral density (PSD) matrix (S(ω)):
(23)
C(τ)=∫−∞∞S(ω)eıωτdω \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{C}(\tau)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \mathcal{S}(\omega) e^{\imath \omega \tau} d \omega \tag{23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
To isolate C(τ) at a particular frequency (ω∗), we apply an idealized narrow band-pass filter centered at ω∗. Specifically, the filter’s transfer function H(ω) is given by,
(24)
H(ω)={1ifω∈A0otherwise \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} H(\omega)= \begin{cases}1 & \text { if } \omega \in A \\ 0 & \text { otherwise }\end{cases} \tag{24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where A = [ω∗ − ϵ, ω∗ + ϵ] ∪ [ − ω∗ − ϵ, − ω∗ + ϵ] and ϵ is a small positive value defining the narrow bandwidth 2ϵ around ω∗. The spectral density matrix of this filtered signal is given by,
(25)
Sfiltered(ω)=|H(ω)|2S(ω) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{\mathcal { S }}_{\text {filtered }}(\omega)=|H(\omega)|^{2} \boldsymbol{\mathcal { S }}(\omega) \tag{25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The resulting autocorrelation of the filtered signal is expressed as:
(26)
Cfiltered(τ)=∫−∞∞Sfiltered(ω)eıωτdω=∫−∞∞|H(ω)|2S(ω)eıωτdω. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \mathrm{C}_{\text {filtered }}(\tau) & =\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \mathcal{S}_{\text {filtered }}(\omega) e^{\imath \omega \tau} d \omega \\ & =\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|H(\omega)|^{2} \mathcal{S}(\omega) e^{\imath \omega \tau} d \omega . \tag{(26} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Using the definition of H(ω) and assuming ϵ to be sufficiently small such that (ω) and eıωτ are approximately constant over the integration intervals of width 2ϵ, the frequency-specific autocorrelation at ω∗ is:
(27)
C(τ)|ω∗≈2ϵ(S(−ω∗)e−ıω∗τ+S(ω∗)eıω∗τ). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left.\mathbf{C}(\tau)\right|_{\omega^{*}} \approx 2 \epsilon\left(\mathcal{S}\left(-\omega^{*}\right) e^{-\imath \omega^{*} \tau}+\mathcal{S}\left(\omega^{*}\right) e^{\imath \omega^{*} \tau}\right) . \tag{(27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
At zero time lag (τ = 0),
(28)
C(0)|ω∗≈2ϵ(S(−ω∗)+S(ω∗)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left.\mathbf{C}(0)\right|_{\omega^{*}} \approx 2 \epsilon\left(\boldsymbol{\mathcal { S }}\left(-\omega^{*}\right)+\boldsymbol{\mathcal { S }}\left(\omega^{*}\right)\right) . \tag{28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since the PSD is Hermitian, i.e., S (ω) = ∗ S (− ω)), we have
(29)
C(0)|ω∗=4ϵRe{S(ω∗)} \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left.\mathbf{C}(0)\right|_{\omega^{*}}=4 \epsilon \operatorname{Re}\left\{\boldsymbol{\mathcal { S }}\left(\omega^{*}\right)\right\} \tag{29} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This frequency-specific autocorrelation matrix (C(0)|ω∗) can then be used in place of C(0) in the communication subspace analysis described above to evaluate the prediction performance at any frequency. As our analysis does not depend on the absolute scaling of this matrix, the prefactor 4ϵ can be disregarded, and we effectively use Re {S (ω∗)} as the frequency-specific correlation matrix.
